WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.750
- Welcome, everyone.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.540
I'm delighted to introduce Duy Lap Nguyen

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.250
who is Associate Professor

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.460
in the Department of Modern
and Classical Languages

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.560
at the University of Houston.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.530
His work, which is at the
intersection of history,

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.960
literature, critical
theory, and philosophy

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.510
breaks down conceptual and
disciplinary boundaries

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.900
that have long limited
the way we think about

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.143
important issues in Vietnamese studies.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.040
He is equally at home
discussing the intricacies

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.250
of Marxist social theory
and historical materialism,

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.600
as well as at revisiting
the intellectual history

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.810
of 20th century Vietnam.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.740
He's not only adept at
unraveling the mysteries

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.450
of Jacques Derrida or Walter Benjamin,

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.470
but at teasing out the
political, historical

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.940
and intellectual significance
of Vietnamese spy novels

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.800
from the late 1960s and early 1970s.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.280
He writes regularly for
journals in critical Marxism,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.230
and also forces us to rethink
the unsophisticated ways

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:13.510
most Vietnam studies
scholars have underestimated

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.420
and flattened the
intellectual complexities

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.580
of Vietnamese Marxist thought

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.230
and social theory within
Vietnam in general.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.630
The results are provocative
and transformative

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:29.280
and show a path for engaging
with the history of ideas

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.330
and cultural productions
like literature, film,

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:35.840
and television, as a
serious intellectual project

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.340
that transforms the world
rather than just serves

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.603
as a derivative aspect
of political history.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.730
Duy takes intellectual history seriously

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.790
as intellectual history.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.530
It's not just a pathway into understanding

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.490
the stories we already
know about politics.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.637
And this is what he is
done with this book,

00:01:56.637 --> 00:01:58.190
"The Unimagined Community:

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.000
Imperialism and Culture
in South Vietnam",

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.690
which he'll be talking about today.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.560
So please join me in
welcoming Duy Lap Nguyen

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:06.780
for today's talk.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:07.613
Thank you.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:13.070
- Thanks, Erik, for that
over-the-top generous introduction,

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.803
which I certainly don't deserve.

00:02:15.821 --> 00:02:20.120
And thanks, Kristine, for
organizing all of this as well.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:22.560
I've been a big fan of this series.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.530
There's been a bunch of
wonderful talks in this series,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.560
so it's a real pleasure
to be able to participate.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.650
Okay, so I'm just gonna jump right into it

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.753
and share my screen with all of you.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:33.600
Okay.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.100
So on September 2nd, 1963,

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.810
Ngo Dinh Nhu, the brother
in chief political advisor

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.170
of the south Vietnamese
president Ngo Dinh Diem

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.490
spoke in secret with a Polish diplomat,

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.520
Mieczyslaw Maneli to
discuss the possibility

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.630
of peace talks with Hanoi.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:53.220
In the context of the
deteriorating relationship

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.880
between the Americans
and the South Vietnamese

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.830
the meeting raised US
suspicions that Nhu was trying

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.223
to betray the Alliance
and forfeit the war.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.870
The crisis was stirred by an incident

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.070
that occurred several months earlier.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:10.210
In May, South Vietnamese
soldiers in the city of Hue

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.850
killed several unarmed civilians
opposing a government ban

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.853
on the public display of Buddhist flags.

00:03:18.275 --> 00:03:20.910
The episode ignited a
wave of political protests

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.530
that would come to be symbolized
by the famous photograph

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.210
of Thich Quang Duc self-immolation,

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.670
shattering the image of
the South Vietnamese state

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:33.090
as a stable, viable and democratic
bastion of the free world.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:36.190
The photograph would
become a universal symbol

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.663
of rebellion in the
fight against injustice.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.840
Taken during the height of
the civil rights movement,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.550
the picture appeared to
provide irrefutable evidence

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.580
that the Republic of Vietnam
was a repressive regime,

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:52.210
denying equality to its
own Buddhist majority.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.310
The backlash in the international press

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:57.990
would prompt US officials to
threaten a withdraw of support

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.810
in a complete suspension
of aid at a moment

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.490
when the insurgency appeared
to be fulfilling its pledge

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.080
to quote, "destroy the
dictatorial government

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.513
of the American lackey", Ngo Dinh Diem.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.750
In less than two months after
the meeting in September

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.890
Diem and Nhu were killed in a coup backed

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.040
by the American Embassy.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.260
The assassination in
November marked the beginning

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.750
of a tragic comic cycle
of changing regimes

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.610
and incompetent leaders.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:27.150
This ended in April, 1975
with the communist victory

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.250
and the humiliating
withdrawal of American forces

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:31.720
after two decades of war

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.230
that left millions of Vietnamese dead.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.400
By that point, the Republic
was already widely regarded

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.130
as a political puppet,

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:42.360
manufactured by the world's
greatest capitalist power,

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.703
waging an imperialist war
against the poor peasant society.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:50.660
So seen from this vantage point,

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.610
news response to Maneli during
their September meeting,

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.313
seems utterly strange and inexplicable.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.747
After a lengthy. quote,

00:04:58.747 --> 00:05:03.220
"exposition on the philosophy
of the cult of personality"

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.040
and its role in what he described

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.598
as the Republic's increasing
success in the war,

00:05:08.598 --> 00:05:12.030
Nhu, unaware that the end had arrived,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:15.810
announced the beginning of
a new phase of the conflict.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.707
For the south Vietnamese,
the war had become a, quote,

00:05:19.707 --> 00:05:22.970
"social revolution" against
the communist revolution

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.860
in the countryside.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.490
As a total transformation
of society, this revolution,

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.900
moreover, was not simply
a nationalist struggle

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:33.980
to overcome communism.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:37.660
On the contrary, nationalism,
according to Nhu,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.820
was a, quote, alibi exploited
by third world leaders

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:45.310
who failed to recognize the
need for social revolution

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:46.793
in underdeveloped countries.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:51.590
Casting the Buddhist affair
as an imperialist plot

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.870
to crush the social revolution,

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:57.070
in the name of a Western
ideal of democracy,

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:58.507
Nhu declared the quote,

00:05:58.507 --> 00:06:00.840
"The war cannot be one with the Americans

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.510
because they are an obstacle

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:05.360
to the revolutionary
transformation of society

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.260
which is the prerequisite for victory."

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.880
This obstacle was identified in particular

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.560
with the mechanism of American aid,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.920
which Nhu had condemned an unmistakably

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.880
Marxist terminology as an instrument

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.560
of underdevelopment, quote,
"Vietnam not a guinea pig

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.090
for capitalist imperialism
to experiment on."

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.700
The new phase of the conflict, therefore,

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.190
would call for resistance,

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:34.340
both against communism and
American neocolonialism.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.830
The Republic as Nhu explained
to an astonished Maneli

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.553
is fighting against both the
guerillas and imperialism.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.200
But even more puzzling than the idea

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.520
that the South Vietnamese were engaged

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.110
in an anti-Imperial struggle
with their American allies

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.020
was the fact that for Nhu the ultimate aim

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.400
of the social revolution in the South

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.920
was not to defeat communism.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:03.860
Rather, the goal was the
abolition of capitalism

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.370
just as the Americans were now an obstacle

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.490
to the revolutionary
transformation of society.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:12.520
So the quote, "capitalist
regime in the South

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.960
cannot effectively oppose communism."

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.750
In another display of this pension

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:21.140
for abstruse dialectical
statements, Nhu, therefore,

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:24.010
explained that the
anti-communist war was in fact,

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:26.327
an anti-capitalist strategy, quote,

00:07:26.327 --> 00:07:29.490
"I am really combating
communism in order to put an end

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.897
to materialistic capitalism."

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.510
So this apparently implausible explanation

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:39.610
of the political aims of the
South Vietnamese government

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:42.840
has been taken as evidence
that Nhu was insane

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.650
as a symptom of his
opium induced paranoia,

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.330
or one of his Machiavellian schemes

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.640
to preserve his own power.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.180
But it's just as implausible

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.990
that the leader of a
reactionary puppet regime

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.290
would try to avoid an eminent coup

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.740
supported by his neo-colonial patrons

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:01.760
by suddenly reinventing
himself as a Marxist opponent

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.520
of US imperialism.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.637
And in fact,

00:08:04.637 --> 00:08:07.960
Nhu had long expressed his
opposition to capitalism,

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:09.770
for example, in the manifesto

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.220
to his Can Lao Revolutionary Party,

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:13.557
where he writes, quote,

00:08:13.557 --> 00:08:16.520
"The capitalists have
trampled on human dignity

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.180
to build a career of wealth

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.057
on the blood of laboring people."

00:08:21.705 --> 00:08:24.320
Nhu's anti capitalist party, moreover,

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:25.900
would play an important role

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:29.660
in establishing Ngo Dinh
Diem's government in 1955,

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:32.210
a government which embraced
a version of socialism

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.353
that was different from
that of the communist party

00:08:34.353 --> 00:08:36.760
with the Vietnamese Workers' Party.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.277
As Nhu explained in an interview, quote,

00:08:39.277 --> 00:08:42.180
"we are working towards
a socialistic state,

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:44.750
a non-Marxist state of free cooperatives

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.397
where labor shares control of industry."

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.840
Like the idea of an anti-capitalist war

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.300
against the Vietnamese Communist Party.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.610
The notion of a non-Marxist socialism

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.650
may seem self-contradictory

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:59.860
given the capitalist/communist binary

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:03.450
that framed geopolitics
during the cold war.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:05.760
And in fact, this led
one observer to argue

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.040
that the First Republic
was not a Catholic regime.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.470
Rather, the Catholicism was merely a cover

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:14.217
for communism pure and simple, quote,

00:09:14.217 --> 00:09:17.400
"Nhu fastened his grip on the
labor movement in Vietnam,"

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.240
and this is a fact that
sometimes forgotten

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:20.570
that he was a political organizer

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:23.520
before he became part
of the First Republic.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.140
And Nhu "conducted the labor
movement under another name,

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.410
the Humanist Workers Revolutionary Party,"

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.697
which is the translation here for Can Lao.

00:09:32.697 --> 00:09:34.670
"Stripped of its flowery rhetoric,

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:37.370
Mr. Nhu's non-Marxist personal Marxism

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.030
should never have been reassuring

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:43.230
the do-good Americans
claim that Ngo Dinh Diem

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:47.380
represented a third force,
hostile to communism.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.720
The specious explanation
made him acceptable

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:52.300
to America's government and press.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.270
In Nhu's case, the whole theory was rot.

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:58.280
Many Vietnamese were in
the third force category,

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:00.700
but Ngo Dinh Nhu was never one of them.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.580
He was among the
intellectuals of the left.

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:06.150
In their game, the devout
Diem was never anything

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:07.750
but a front."

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:11.530
So this non-Marxist personal Marxism,

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.350
which was the official
doctrine of the First Republic,

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:16.350
is the same as what Maneli called

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.480
the philosophy of the cult to personality.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:21.110
The theory informing the strategy

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:23.450
that the Republic was supposedly using

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.540
to fight the insurgency and
to resist the Americans,

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.113
the Strategic Hamlet Campaign.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:32.170
So is there some validity

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:36.110
to Ngo Dinh Nhu's apparently
implausible account of the war?

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:39.040
Did the coup in 1963 marked the demise

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.840
of a corrupt puppet regime,

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:43.290
pretending to be a
parliamentary government,

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:46.710
or could it have been the
defeat of a social revolution

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:49.120
against the communist
insurgency in the south

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:50.683
and US imperialism?

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:54.330
So this is a question
that I explore in my book,

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:55.590
the "The Unimagined Community:

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:58.640
Imperialism and Culture
in South Vietnam".

00:10:58.640 --> 00:10:59.530
And in my talk today,

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:02.310
which is taken from the
first part of my book,

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:05.370
I wanna reconstruct the lengthy exposition

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.840
on personalism and counterinsurgency,

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:10.983
which Nhu relayed?? to Maneli in 1963.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.780
So what was this non-Marxist
personalist Marxism

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.970
that Nhu described as the philosophy

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.210
of the Strategic Hamlet Campaign?

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:22.760
And did the Nhu's actually
conceive of the program

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:25.990
as an anti-capitalist
war against communism?

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:27.350
And finally, what would

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:30.233
such a seemingly
contradictory project entail?

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:33.650
So to give you an outline
of my presentation today,

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:37.316
the first part will examine
the theory of personalism.

00:11:37.316 --> 00:11:40.100
And I argue here that personalism

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.330
was not a reactionary religious ideology,

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.660
but a radical anti capitalist theory

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:48.903
that also included a critique
of bourgeois democracy.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.360
This critique informed
the Nhu's understanding

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.750
of the impact of colonialism.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.350
And the Nhu's perspective
is interesting here

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:58.470
because it departs

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:01.870
from the conventional postcolonial
account of colonialism

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.680
as being this hypocritical enterprise

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.890
that preached the rights of
man denying them in practice.

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:11.280
For the Nhu's, the
introduction of capitalism

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.760
and the liberalization
of law under the French,

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:17.200
acting in accordance
with the rights of man,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:20.830
undermine the communal form of
democracy or village autonomy

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:24.420
that constituted the basis
of Vietnamese civilization.

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:27.460
And then in the last
part of the presentation,

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:30.270
I argue that it was this
communal form of democracy

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.380
that the Nhu's were trying to restore

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:35.683
in a modernized form through
the Strategic Hamlet Campaign.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.760
The program was not simply
a counterinsurgency measure,

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.490
but rather, a full scale social revolution

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:47.260
aimed at democratizing rural
South Vietnamese society

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:51.150
while organizing the peasantry
into rural popular bases.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:53.150
And these bases were supposed to serve

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:54.440
three real related purposes:

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:57.240
first, to contain the communist insurgency

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:00.060
using a version of the
communist people's war;

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:03.200
second, to isolate the
comprador capitalist class

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:06.250
in the cities; and three,
to limit the interference

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:08.830
of the American government
in Vietnamese affairs.

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:09.663
Okay.

00:13:09.663 --> 00:13:13.433
So part one, Theory of Personalism:
Reactionary or Radical?

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.080
So when I began writing this book,

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.560
I kept coming across statements

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:20.260
by the early South Vietnamese leaders

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:23.380
that seemed to affirm Marxist ideas,

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:25.710
which seemed strange given
the conventional account

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:29.230
of the First Republic is
reactionary puppet regime.

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:32.030
So for example, Nhu, during
his meeting with Maneli

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:35.330
described the ultimate
aims of the Republic

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:36.280
in the following manner.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.100
He said, "That is right.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.370
I agree with Marx's final conclusion:

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:42.970
the state must wither away."

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:44.640
And then at a planning session

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:47.290
for the Strategic Hamlet Campaign,

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:48.487
Nhu emphasized what he called

00:13:48.487 --> 00:13:51.310
"the first science of
organizing mass movements

00:13:51.310 --> 00:13:53.330
based in the Soviet organization

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.104
of the communist in Russia."

00:13:55.104 --> 00:13:59.125
(Duy Lap speaking in foreign language)

00:13:59.125 --> 00:14:02.410
So what do the Russian Soviet councils

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:05.460
and Marxist ideas like the
withering away of the state

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:09.240
have to do with the reactionary
theory of personalism?

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.350
So to try to find some
answers to this question,

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.890
I went back to one of the primary sources

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.610
of Vietnamese personalism
which is the work

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:19.450
of the French Catholic
philosopher Emmanuel Mounier.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:21.630
And Mounier actually was considered

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.540
one of the most important
philosophers in France

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.173
during the '40s and '50s.

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:30.120
In the literature on the Vietnam war,

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:35.450
this work is often characterized
as advocating a "third way"

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:36.980
between "Marxist collectivism

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:39.427
and Western individualistic capitalism."

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:42.700
So John Donnell describes the doctrine

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:46.240
as an "unsystematic melange
of 'third force' ideas,"

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.040
seeking a "middle-ground"

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:50.363
between communism and liberal democracy.

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:54.260
But as the French philosopher
Lucien Seve noted,

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:56.930
the third way that Mounier
proposed was in fact

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:59.150
much more directly aligned with Marxism

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:00.280
than liberal democracy.

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:03.400
Quote, "what characterizes
Mounier's third way

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:06.560
is not that it is situated
at an equal distance

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:08.240
between two polls.

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:12.040
On the contrary, it is
much closer her to Marxism.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:14.890
The factor that unites currents
like the existentialism

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:18.480
of Sartre and his disciples
and the personalism of Mounier

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.480
and his followers is precisely

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:24.110
that they define themselves
with reference to Marxism."

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:27.970
So as Seve's description
suggests, Mounier's personalism

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:31.370
can be understood as a form
of Marxist humanist thought,

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:34.640
a category more often associated
with the work of thinkers

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:38.473
like Sartre and Lukacs and Henri Lefebvre.

00:15:38.473 --> 00:15:40.960
Mounier's personalism, therefore,

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:44.290
as Nhu pointed out in an
interview with Le Monde

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:47.300
was not a conservative
ideology, but a radical one.

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:50.900
Quote, "We are a reactionary
regime you've been told?"

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:52.757
said Nhu sarcastically.

00:15:52.757 --> 00:15:55.280
"Are you aware of the fact
that we take our inspiration

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:57.510
from the thinkers of the Western left?

00:15:57.510 --> 00:15:58.580
But you must realize

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:00.580
that we based ourselves on personalism!"

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:07.090
Now, the concept of the person
in personalism is related

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:11.370
to both the humanist and Marxist
aspects of Mounier's work.

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:15.330
So in this work, the person
is defined as the foundation

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.090
for a philosophical anthropology.

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:20.370
The person is the ontological basis

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:24.010
of all knowledge and action
assuming the position of God

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:25.460
within Christian theology.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:28.990
Quote "the person stands
at the summit of being,

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:33.640
and all being is subject to
a process of personalization.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:35.450
Hence, the metaphysics of the person

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:39.350
will provide the ultimate
criteria for all praxis.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:42.220
Mounier's philosophy then is a humanism

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:44.210
because it tries to explain
everything beginning

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:47.163
with the principle of the
human being or the person.

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:52.030
But Mounier also opposes
the concept of the person

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:55.513
to the egoistic individual
within bourgeois society.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.680
In a capitalist economy,
according to Mounier,

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.470
an inversion occurs in which the freedom

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:05.730
of self-interested individuals gives rise

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.803
to what he called the tyranny
of production for profit.

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.520
As a result of the free
exchange of commodities

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.790
between individuals money,

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:17.620
which serves as a means at exchange,

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:20.170
becomes the aim of production itself,

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:23.880
subordinating persons to the
impersonal power of capital.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.880
Quote, "under capitalism, it is not money

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:29.770
that is at the service of
the economy and people.

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:33.150
It is people who are at
the service of money.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:37.160
For Mounier then the capitalist
economy is characterized

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:41.370
by a process of dehumanization
or depersonalization,

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:43.170
through which the people become dominated

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:47.330
by the impersonal power of
things, money and capital.

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:49.430
Subjected to what Mounier describes

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.300
with reference to the Marxist conception

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:54.097
of commodity fetishism, as the quote,

00:17:54.097 --> 00:17:56.860
"mystifications into which
man has been inveigled

00:17:56.860 --> 00:17:58.340
by social constructions

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:00.960
derived from his material conditions."

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:03.910
Humanity is ruled by a sovereign economy

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:06.807
that humanity itself has created, quote,

00:18:06.807 --> 00:18:09.900
"it is not the economy that
is at the service of man,

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:13.490
but man who is at the
service of the economy."

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:17.380
So here, if you simply
replace depersonalization

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:19.560
with reification, then you get something

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.863
that looks pretty close to
Lukacs's criticism of capitalism.

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:28.128
And in the manifesto of the
Can Lao Revolutionary Party,

00:18:28.128 --> 00:18:31.810
Nhu appears to incorporate this
concept of depersonalization

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:34.370
into the very platform of the party.

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:37.550
Affirming the principle
of personalist socialism,

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:40.900
Nhu declared the quote,
"production must serve the people

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:43.690
in contrast to the inhuman
condition in capitalism,

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:46.827
where it is man who is at
the service of the economy."

00:18:48.252 --> 00:18:50.520
In Mounier's writings, moreover,

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:53.190
this depersonalized economy is described

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:55.700
as the infrastructure
of bourgeois democracy

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:57.297
or parliamentary government.

00:18:57.297 --> 00:19:01.197
And Mounier rejected the latter
in unequivocal terms, quote,

00:19:01.197 --> 00:19:04.770
"we hold all manner of
liberalism in horror,

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:08.540
for it is the greatest and
most evil fraud in history.

00:19:08.540 --> 00:19:11.410
The most cunning and cruel hoax of all."

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:16.230
And this hoax was a legacy
of the revolution of 1789,

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:19.630
which according to Mounier
was not a popular revolution,

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:21.263
but a bourgeois revolution.

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:24.900
The function of this
bourgeois form of democracy

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.470
that was established by
the French revolution

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:32.170
was to ratify the despotic
rule of the capitalist economy.

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:34.060
In this political system,

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:37.680
the state does not represent
the people as such,

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:41.770
but rather, the universal
interest of atomized individuals

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:44.650
whose free interaction in the marketplace

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:47.343
creates the tyranny of
production for profit.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:49.750
In Mounier's terms,

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:52.497
the role of the state
under capitalism is, quote,

00:19:52.497 --> 00:19:55.420
"restricted to the
assurance that these egoisms

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:58.470
should not encroach upon one
another or on their betterment

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.850
as a purely profit making association."

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:03.190
Although Mounier, moreover,

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:05.610
was critical of the Soviet Union,

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.150
his critique of the latter
was not that it failed

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:10.030
to incorporate the positive elements

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:12.600
of capitalism and liberal democracy.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:13.710
On the contrary,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:15.570
the problem was that it was incapable

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:18.110
of completely abolishing capitalism

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.200
and the parliamentary form of democracy

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:23.380
that constituted its
political super-structure.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:25.940
For Mounier, in fact, the Soviet system

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:29.450
was merely a collectivized
version of capitalism,

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:31.830
one in which the centralized apparatus

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:34.530
of what he called the massive Soviet state

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:38.400
organizes the exploitation
of proletarianized labor

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:41.333
in the place of the capitalist class.

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:47.013
So echoing Marx's famous
formula for social revolution

00:20:47.013 --> 00:20:50.470
that quote, "changes in the
economic foundation lead

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:53.310
to the transformation
of the superstructure,"

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:54.857
Mounier argued, the quote,

00:20:54.857 --> 00:20:58.000
"the liberal stage of
democracy will be superseded

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.967
only on the foundation of
a new social structure."

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:05.700
In contrast to the Soviet
Union, this new social structure

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:08.260
would not be a centralized government,

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:11.780
but rather what Mounier called
a communitarian organization

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:14.300
of decentralized communities.

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.660
Liberated from both the capitalist tyranny

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:18.470
of production for profit,

00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:21.610
as well as from the
dictatorship of the proletariat,

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:25.250
which perpetuates the capitalist
institution of wage labor,

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:28.850
quote, "By socialism,
we mean the following:

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:31.110
the abolition of the proletarian condition

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:34.337
and the supersession of the
anarchic economy of profit."

00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:36.333
Okay.

00:21:36.333 --> 00:21:38.983
So part II: Colonialism and Liberalism.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:43.530
This critique of capitalism
in Bourgeois Individualism

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:46.030
was adopted by the leaders
of the First Republic,

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:50.170
thus in a speech Tran
LE Xuan or Madame Nhu

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:51.567
declared the, quote,

00:21:51.567 --> 00:21:54.510
"Our union will never be
a disordered collection

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:56.550
of individuals each following

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:58.920
the sole aim of personal interest.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:03.240
Our union draws its strength
from unity and discipline."

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:04.830
So this personalist union

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:07.500
was opposed then to the market economy,

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:08.870
which according to Mounier

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:11.960
is based on an egoistic conception

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:15.430
of man as the, quote, "ideology
and prevailing structure

00:22:15.430 --> 00:22:17.100
of Western bourgeois society

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.303
in the 18th and 19th centuries.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:24.740
Man in the abstract, unattached
to any natural community,

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:29.330
the sovereign lord of a liberty
unlimited and undirected."

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:32.580
In the Vietnamese
personalism developed by Nhu,

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.610
Mounier's depiction of bourgeois
society is appropriated

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:39.097
as a critique of Western
civilization itself, quote,

00:22:39.097 --> 00:22:42.880
"Occidental countries had for
their structure capitalism,

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.880
for their ideology liberalism."

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:48.450
For Nhu then, parliamentary government

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:50.190
is the political super structure

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:52.400
of a capitalist mode??/motor/ production,

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:54.913
which he associated
with Western societies.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:58.400
In these societies, according to Mounier,

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:01.700
the principle of formal equality
and the abstract freedom

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:06.500
of egoistic individuals
conceal the concrete inequality

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:09.663
created by an economy based
on production for profit.

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:14.940
So this critique of capitalism
as a de-personalized society

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:17.070
of self-interested individuals

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:20.810
subordinated to the impersonal
rule of the economy,

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:23.160
informed the critique of colonialism,

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:24.340
that Nhu develop as part

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:26.587
of his Vietnamese personalism, quote,

00:23:26.587 --> 00:23:28.620
"Under colonial domination,

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:31.300
man is subjected to money and power.

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:34.610
Such a regime favors the
egoistic type of man.

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:37.650
Against colonialism, the
Vietnamese Personalist

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:40.880
and communitarian revolution
conceives of human value

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:45.510
as independent of money and
residing in responsibility."

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:47.400
In the early colonial period,

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:50.900
the imposition of capitalism
was accompanied by an ambitious

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:53.670
French liberalization of indigenous law,

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:57.440
which introduced the individual
as a legal abstraction.

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:00.110
Instead of refusing to
recognize the humanity

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:02.993
of its Vietnamese subjects,
as the story usually goes,

00:24:04.410 --> 00:24:06.100
the early colonial administration

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:09.240
attempted to grant them
their inalienable rights,

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:11.890
rights which had been
denied by the Imperial court

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:13.853
in its tyrannical regime.

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:17.210
Embracing what Alexander
Woodside referred to

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:19.640
as a social revolutionism,

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:22.530
the early colonialist conceived
to be Imperial project

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.630
as an extension of the French Revolution.

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:29.100
In the colony, therefore, the
rights of man were applied

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.180
in order to limit the political tyranny

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:32.670
of the Imperial state,

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:36.500
as well as the domestic tyranny
of the traditional family.

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:39.210
As the early colonialists
discovered, however,

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:41.910
the attempt to establish
these inalienable rights

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:45.110
would weaken the very foundation
of the non-Western society

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:47.587
they intend to liberate, quote,

00:24:47.587 --> 00:24:50.290
"In traditional Vietnam, the individual"

00:24:50.290 --> 00:24:52.317
as James Barnhart has argued

00:24:52.317 --> 00:24:54.400
"was not really ontologically separate

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:56.010
from his or her family.

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:58.980
The notion of the
distinct civic individual,

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:02.047
the fundament of all
Western law was absent."

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:05.547
By applying what Marx
referred to as the, quote,

00:25:05.547 --> 00:25:09.790
"so called rights of man"
separated from the community,

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:10.750
the French, therefore,

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:14.270
would unwitingly erode the
traditional bonds of community

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.453
that prevailed in the precolonial period.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:21.210
During this period, the
country consisted of a myriad

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:23.810
of scattered semi-autonomous communes

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:26.380
governed by the Imperial court

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:28.140
whose seemingly absolute power

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:30.950
was in fact largely spectacular

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:33.830
limited to the ritual
exercises of sovereignty,

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:37.160
the coronation, the public
execution and so on,

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:39.100
as Ta Van Tai suggests.

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:41.230
The Imperial state was a sovereignty

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:44.330
that was almost completely
devoid of discipline

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:47.570
in the Foucauldian sense of the
latter as the daily exercise

00:25:47.570 --> 00:25:51.100
of surveillance, operating
invisibly at the everyday level,

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:53.610
quote, "the Vietnamese diagnostic state

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:57.030
was a relatively weak
polity, without the ability

00:25:57.030 --> 00:25:59.820
to reach deeply into all spheres of life,

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:02.380
because it did not have at its disposal

00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:05.520
mass communications means
and a large police force

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:07.590
or prison system."

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:09.980
Owing to the weakness
of the Imperial court

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:13.447
and its tyrannical rule,
village notables, quote,

00:26:13.447 --> 00:26:14.780
"manage local affairs,

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:16.800
and the family was the fundamental unit

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:18.890
of disciplinary jurisdiction.

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:20.490
Early French visitors were struck

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:22.980
by the remarkably decentralized nature

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.597
of social organization."

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:27.570
In the precolonial period,

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:29.950
the family in the semi-autonomous village

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:32.760
exercised a discipline
that preempted the role

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:34.930
of the centralized government,

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:38.450
lacking the disciplinary
apparatus of the modern state.

00:26:38.450 --> 00:26:41.730
The despotism of this weak polity then

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:44.510
was never extended over
the entire territory,

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:47.260
nominally belonging to the emperor.

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:51.150
This weakness is expressed
in the traditional adage

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:52.959
(Duy Lap speaks in foreign language)

00:26:52.959 --> 00:26:54.610
"the right of the emperor gives way

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:56.840
to the customs of the village."

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:59.290
In the precolonial period, therefore,

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:01.410
a despotic centralized state,

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:03.840
which lacked the power of discipline,

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:06.130
presided over a decentralized form

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:10.290
of directly associated
production based on the family.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:13.350
As a Republic of Vietnam
government publication describes,

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:17.120
quote, "the social and political
organization of Vietnam

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:21.440
consisted of two opposing
and superimposed systems.

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.600
At the bottom there was
popular autonomous democracy,

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:27.070
at the top was the absolute monarchy,

00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:30.747
which was highly centralized
thanks to its Mandarin system."

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:35.180
The Vietnamese nation, therefore,
was like a confederation

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:38.190
composed of numerous small
communal governments,

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:41.480
autonomous village communes,
which sprung up spontaneously

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:44.640
and adapted themselves to the
extremely centralized system

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:47.327
of the, quote, "Oriental
absolute monarchies."

00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:52.280
Vietnamese society, as
Ellen hammer described,

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:55.920
was characterized by both a
highly developed autocracy

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:58.693
and a remarkable degree of democracy.

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:02.360
Now in terms of the
relationship between the base

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:05.730
and the super structure,
between the economic foundation

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:07.870
and the legal and political order,

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:09.930
the system was exactly the opposite

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:12.300
of the one imposed by the French.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.480
In bourgeois democracy,
the formal equality

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:18.590
guaranteed by the state
conceals the existence

00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:21.730
of concrete inequality
within civil society,

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:23.820
a contradiction that, according to Marx,

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:25.773
is characteristic of capitalism.

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:29.540
In contrast, the absolute sovereignty,

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:31.220
according to the Vietnamese emperor,

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:34.180
was merely a formalized tyranny,

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:37.100
in abstraction disguising
the concrete weakness

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:40.120
of Imperial power in the
democratic organization

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:41.890
of communal production.

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:46.130
In used terms, the official
autocracy of the Imperial state

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:50.320
was limited in reality by
the, quote, "democratic nature

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:53.345
of the autonomous organization
(speaks in foreign language),

00:28:53.345 --> 00:28:54.270
of the village

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:57.220
and its independence in relation
to the central government.

00:28:58.510 --> 00:29:02.070
Whereas the democratic rights
that the liberal state grants

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:05.427
to its citizens are
abrogated, as Mounier argued,

00:29:05.427 --> 00:29:08.100
"by the facts of their
economic existence,"

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:11.300
the despotic authority of the
Vietnamese court was abrogated

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:14.780
by the economic autonomy
of its Imperial subjects.

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:16.110
The rights of the emperor

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:19.070
give way to the customs of the village.

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:22.870
If the emperor then exercise
in absolute sovereignty

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:26.130
over his subjects, this
formalized privilege

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:29.240
was subordinate
nevertheless to the village

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:31.673
and its extrajuridical customs.

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:36.230
In contrast, therefore, to
the legal right of the king,

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:39.500
the quote, "democracy and
autonomy in the village,"

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:42.824
as Diem pointed out,
"was not a written law,

00:29:42.824 --> 00:29:44.390
(Duy Lap speaks in foreign language)

00:29:44.390 --> 00:29:46.555
rather it was a "customary practice

00:29:46.555 --> 00:29:48.138
(Duy Lap speaks in foreign language)

00:29:48.138 --> 00:29:51.010
passed down from one
generation to the next."

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:52.930
An extrajuridical form of autonomy

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:55.000
that preempted the people subjection

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:57.050
under the law of the emperor.

00:29:57.050 --> 00:30:00.750
But as such the autonomy
of the emperor subjects

00:30:00.750 --> 00:30:03.780
in relation to his
nominally absolute power

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:07.340
cannot be identified with the
inalienable right of freedom,

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:09.880
belonging to an abstract individual.

00:30:09.880 --> 00:30:11.920
In Europe, as Diem pointed out,

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:14.300
the establishment of
this juridical freedom

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:17.970
had coincided historically
with a development of new forms

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:20.090
of coercion in techniques of control.

00:30:20.090 --> 00:30:24.390
Quote, "in liberalism,
discipline is indispensable.

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:26.480
All economic activity is subject

00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:28.010
to the exigencies of technique

00:30:28.010 --> 00:30:30.490
which is imposed upon each
of us and prevents him

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:31.867
from acting independently."

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:36.340
During the era of industrial capitalism,

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:38.500
these disciplinary
techniques were deployed

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:40.957
in Foucault's terms for
the purpose of, quote,

00:30:40.957 --> 00:30:44.170
"extracting time
and labor from bodies."

00:30:44.170 --> 00:30:48.360
Adapted to the colonial context,
the new forms of discipline

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:50.477
provided the French with, quote,

00:30:50.477 --> 00:30:53.120
"capacities to control and to coerce

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:56.290
never dreamed of by
previous Vietnamese rulers,"

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:59.130
which meant that traditional
village obligations

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:03.220
to the ruler, taxes, corvee
service, and military service,

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:06.660
could now be enforced with
unprecedented efficiency."

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:10.220
On the other hand, in
the precolonial period,

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:11.827
as Diem pointed out, quote,

00:31:11.827 --> 00:31:14.750
"there was never an excessive
centralization of power

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:16.790
to enable conscription of labor-power"

00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:18.980
on a continual basis.

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:20.580
Lacking the power to impose

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:22.980
a "daily exercise of surveillance,"

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:26.187
the "Annamite state," as one
French observer acknowledged,

00:31:26.187 --> 00:31:28.673
"intervenes only on rare occasions.

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:31.807
It is indolent by nature."

00:31:32.680 --> 00:31:36.370
And as Nhu pointed out, this
system of imperial tyranny

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:39.970
and peasant autonomy, underlying
Vietnamese civilization,

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:44.340
was undermined by the political
and economic liberalization

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:46.690
carried out by the colonial regime.

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:50.300
While the imposition of
capitalism and individual rights

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:52.630
liberated the people
from the personal rule

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:55.030
of a weak tyrannical sovereign,

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:58.160
it also subjected the peasantry
to the impersonal reign

00:31:58.160 --> 00:31:58.993
of the market.

00:31:58.993 --> 00:32:01.717
Quote, "The French," according to Nhu,

00:32:01.717 --> 00:32:03.400
"have broken up the organization

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:04.942
of our social infrastructure",

00:32:04.942 --> 00:32:08.670
(speaks in foreign language)
introducing capitalism

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:10.830
in order to "replace an economic system

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:12.677
based on the traditional village,"

00:32:12.677 --> 00:32:15.937
and the "democratic nature of
this autonomous organization."

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:22.060
So this personalist Marxist
account of colonialism

00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:26.070
underlies the Nhu's opposition
to liberal democracy,

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:29.000
which they viewed as a
legacy of the French.

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:32.100
For Nhu, the problem with
attempting to establish

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:34.230
a parliamentary state in Vietnam

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:36.787
was not that the Vietnamese
lacked the, quote,

00:32:36.787 --> 00:32:39.290
"indigenous democratic tradition,"

00:32:39.290 --> 00:32:42.170
having known only what General
Maxwell Taylor referred to

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:45.550
as the, quote, "oriental
despotic tradition."

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:48.640
Rather, the problem was
that this despotic tradition

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:52.360
was based on an entirely
different form of democracy,

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:53.800
one that was directly opposed

00:32:53.800 --> 00:32:56.780
to that of a liberal democratic regime.

00:32:56.780 --> 00:33:00.223
The Ngos, therefore, called
for the creation of a, quote,

00:33:01.717 --> 00:33:03.770
"real democracy from the base

00:33:05.270 --> 00:33:07.940
against formal and liberal democracy,"

00:33:07.940 --> 00:33:12.530
a grassroots democracy created
through "communitarian labor

00:33:12.530 --> 00:33:15.490
based on a social plan,
rather than submission

00:33:15.490 --> 00:33:17.470
to the capitalist order."

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:20.740
This real democracy would restore
were the communal autonomy

00:33:20.740 --> 00:33:23.520
of the Vietnamese village
in the modernized form

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:27.320
of a personalist system of
decentralized communities.

00:33:27.320 --> 00:33:31.630
Now, insofar Diem regarded
this ancient tradition

00:33:31.630 --> 00:33:33.570
as a sort of pre-modern equivalent

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:37.010
to the self-governing form
of the personalist commune,

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:41.320
his traditionalism paradoxically
may had put at him closer

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:44.563
ideologically to communism
than republicanism.

00:33:45.930 --> 00:33:49.460
But if that is the case,
then the first Republic,

00:33:49.460 --> 00:33:51.840
contrary to the conventional view,

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:53.870
was not an authoritarian government

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:56.653
pretending to be a bastion
of liberal democracy.

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:02.080
On the contrary, following
Mounier's Marxist prescription,

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:05.070
the leaders of the early
Republic sought to transform

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:08.430
the parliamentary state
in order to establish

00:34:08.430 --> 00:34:10.350
a communitarian form of democracy,

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:14.130
quote, "the personalism that I advocate

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:17.400
is a militant democracy aimed at modifying

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:19.750
the super structure of
the present government."

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:23.670
Now out this personalist
communitarian revolution

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:27.070
was not simply an empty slogan or phrase,

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:30.070
rather the Ngos tried to
carry out the revolution

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:33.250
in a program called the
Strategic Hamlet Campaign,

00:34:33.250 --> 00:34:34.800
which is usually characterized

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:36.723
as a counterinsurgency strategy.

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:40.030
For the Ngos, however,

00:34:40.030 --> 00:34:43.240
the program was not a
tool of pacification,

00:34:43.240 --> 00:34:45.880
rather the campaign,
which the Ngos defined

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:48.610
as the primary objective
of their government

00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:52.370
was intended as a revolution
aimed at transforming

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:54.830
South Vietnamese society into a system

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:57.653
of semi-autonomous
self-governing communes.

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.250
Once established, these
decentralized communities

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:02.770
would produce what they called

00:35:02.770 --> 00:35:06.730
a revision of the central
government agencies in Saigon

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:08.283
inherited from the French.

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:12.523
So this is Ngo Dinh Nhu
describing that process.

00:35:12.523 --> 00:35:16.606
(man speaks in foreign language)

00:35:17.584 --> 00:35:22.084
(Ngo Dinh speaks in foreign language)

00:35:34.906 --> 00:35:37.510
So here Nhu is distinguishing

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:40.190
between his communitarian
brand of democracy

00:35:40.190 --> 00:35:41.853
and electoral democracy.

00:35:42.940 --> 00:35:45.400
And in the unimagined community,

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:48.060
I argue that this distinction
is vital to understanding

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:49.970
the first Republic as well as the reasons

00:35:49.970 --> 00:35:51.720
for why it collapsed.

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:54.190
The difference between
these two kinds of democracy

00:35:54.190 --> 00:35:55.933
is often elided.

00:35:56.990 --> 00:35:59.990
And the elision underlies
opposing accounts of the conflict.

00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:01.883
On the one hand...

00:36:01.883 --> 00:36:04.743
I'm sorry, opposing accounts
of the queue in 1963.

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:09.120
On the one hand, this event
is portrayed as a consequence

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:11.360
of Diem's authoritarianism,

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.890
of his failure to unify
the Vietnamese people

00:36:13.890 --> 00:36:17.410
by democratically broadening
his base of support.

00:36:17.410 --> 00:36:20.980
Conversely, others have argued
that Diem's authoritarianism

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:23.240
was necessary in order to keep the country

00:36:23.240 --> 00:36:25.750
from collapsing into rivaling factions

00:36:25.750 --> 00:36:28.310
incapable of uniting the South.

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:30.410
In these opposing perspectives,

00:36:30.410 --> 00:36:32.770
Diem's rejection of liberal democracy

00:36:32.770 --> 00:36:36.140
is taken as a rejection
of democracy as such.

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:37.720
Historians differ, however,

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:40.410
on whether Diem's undemocratic
rule was necessary

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:43.663
for fighting the war, or whether
it undermined the Republic.

00:36:44.550 --> 00:36:46.470
I argue that these opposite views

00:36:46.470 --> 00:36:50.260
conceal a more complicated picture.

00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:53.290
Diem was trying to
suppress liberal democracy,

00:36:53.290 --> 00:36:56.280
while massively expanding
the communitarian form

00:36:57.147 --> 00:36:59.810
of the latter espoused by his
personalist was government.

00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:03.740
This was the aim after all of
the Strategic Hamlet Campaign,

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:07.730
quote, "Our Government aims
at one major objective:

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:11.540
the establishment of a truly
democratic infrastructure."

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:14.200
Although the Ngos then
were openly disdainful

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:17.520
of the electoral machinery
of the parliamentary state,

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:19.920
they were also committed the creation

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:22.603
of what they called a
democracy at the base.

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:26.820
Thus Nhu, who rejected
liberal democracy, quote,

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:28.770
reprimanded provincial and district chiefs

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:30.950
for not holding honest elections

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:33.560
for chiefs of strategic hamlets."

00:37:33.560 --> 00:37:36.160
As Nhu explained, "Free, secret elections

00:37:36.160 --> 00:37:37.780
of the hamlet chief and hamlet council

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:40.430
are the key to the success
of the strategic hamlets."

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:44.430
By providing the means
with which to institute

00:37:44.430 --> 00:37:47.920
what he called "basic
democracy on a local scale

00:37:47.920 --> 00:37:51.170
which the people can
understand," the Hamlet Campaign

00:37:51.170 --> 00:37:53.610
would preempt the role of
the parliamentary state

00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:58.060
and its centralized form
of electoral democracy,

00:37:58.060 --> 00:38:03.060
quote, "Through the
Strategic Hamlet Program,

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:05.690
the Government intends to
give back to the hamlets

00:38:05.690 --> 00:38:07.860
the right of self-government

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:11.000
with its own charter and
system of community law."

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:13.760
Because of the program,
hamlet leaders, quote,

00:38:13.760 --> 00:38:14.840
for "the first time,"

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:17.827
as a senior US pacification
adviser observed,

00:38:17.827 --> 00:38:20.937
"were selected by ballot
in most of South Vietnam."

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:25.690
For the Ngos, moreover, the
democratization of the villages

00:38:25.690 --> 00:38:28.660
was as vital to the
security of the country

00:38:28.660 --> 00:38:33.660
as their suppression of
their political opposition,

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:38.430
whom they viewed as members belonging

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:41.300
to an elite urban minority
that had little support

00:38:41.300 --> 00:38:44.530
from the majority of the
Vietnamese in the countryside.

00:38:44.530 --> 00:38:47.210
Thus, while Diem argued that
parliamentary government

00:38:47.210 --> 00:38:51.033
must be suspended during
a state of emergency,

00:38:51.033 --> 00:38:53.460
Nhu pointed out that the
war could only be won

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:56.960
by implementing in every
hamlet in south Vietnam.

00:38:56.960 --> 00:39:00.997
Quote, "Your press" as Diem
explained in an interview,

00:39:00.997 --> 00:39:03.840
"mocks the idea of respect for authority

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:06.710
and glorifies so-called civil liberties

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:08.940
and the need for political opposition.

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:11.340
But this country is in a
life and death struggle.

00:39:11.340 --> 00:39:14.190
And even Western democracy
suspend civil liberties

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:17.610
during will war emergencies."

00:39:17.610 --> 00:39:20.917
As Nhu explained, on
the other hand, quote,

00:39:20.917 --> 00:39:23.180
"in time of war, people think

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:25.840
that you must suppress democracy to win.

00:39:25.840 --> 00:39:28.860
To the contrary, we shall
use the war against communism

00:39:28.860 --> 00:39:30.623
to introduce democracy,

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:36.070
and we shall use democracy to win the war.

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:38.120
This is the strategic concept.

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:41.050
Free elections of the hamlet
chief and hamlet council

00:39:41.050 --> 00:39:42.857
by secret ballot are key."

00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:46.800
So these two statements
are not contradictory.

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:49.490
The Republic was using
the state of emergency

00:39:49.490 --> 00:39:53.310
in an authoritarian manner
to restrict an urban minority

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:55.680
from exercising democratic control

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:58.890
over this central government
agencies in Saigon.

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:01.720
At the same time, it was
implementing a program

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:03.410
of communal decentralization

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:05.780
throughout the rest of the country.

00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:09.150
These two policies moreover
were directly related.

00:40:09.150 --> 00:40:12.730
The Ngos conceived to the
program of rural democratization,

00:40:12.730 --> 00:40:15.260
which they called the
Strategic Hamlet Campaign

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:16.830
as a kind of class struggle

00:40:16.830 --> 00:40:20.720
against the French trained
elite in the cities.

00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:23.250
In contrast to the rural population

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:26.610
who had supported the
anticolonial war, this minority,

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:30.467
according to the Tran Ngoc
Chau, consisted of, quote,

00:40:30.467 --> 00:40:34.810
"bureaucrats, military
officers, and business people,

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:38.160
the majority of whom had
collaborated with the French.

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:40.950
When the French left Vietnam,
the French collaborators

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:43.140
became friends with the Americans.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:45.480
What the Americans knew
about south Vietnam

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:48.090
they knew mainly from these Vietnamese.

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:50.400
It's not surprising, therefore,
that these Vietnamese

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:54.480
were also the ones to benefit
the most from American aid,

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:56.940
particularly the
multi-million dollar program

00:40:56.940 --> 00:41:00.160
to build of South
Vietnamese military forces.

00:41:00.160 --> 00:41:04.170
Meanwhile, in rural South
Vietnam, nothing much changed.

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:05.890
The peasants continued to be exploited

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:07.820
while the Americans were naively preparing

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:10.507
the South Vietnamese to
win a conventional war."

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:14.930
As Chau suggests then, many
among the elite in the city

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:18.250
subscribed to the formula
recommended by the Americans

00:41:18.250 --> 00:41:19.520
for winning the war:

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:22.800
build up the national army to
fight a conventional conflict

00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:24.170
while liberalizing the government

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:27.360
in order to unify the
south Vietnamese people.

00:41:27.360 --> 00:41:29.283
As the Ngos recognized, however,

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:33.010
the aid program that benefited the elite

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:35.320
had created what Philip Catton described

00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:37.920
as a kind of gilded underdevelopment,

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:40.880
one in which the abundance
of consumer commodities

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:42.310
concealed the destruction

00:41:42.310 --> 00:41:44.340
of the country's
industrial infrastructure.

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:46.810
Quote, "the importation of consumer,

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:49.140
rather than capital,
goods created the illusion

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:51.660
of prosperity while
hampering the development

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:52.937
of local industry."

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:56.830
As sir, Robert Thompson noted, moreover,

00:41:56.830 --> 00:42:00.170
the US commitment to maintaining
a costly conventional force

00:42:00.170 --> 00:42:02.890
that was incapable of
fighting an irregular war

00:42:02.890 --> 00:42:06.190
against the insurgency would
only increase the problems

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:08.330
produced by the commodity import program,

00:42:08.330 --> 00:42:11.790
quote, "To pay the recurrent expenditure

00:42:11.790 --> 00:42:14.350
on the armed forces, the
South Vietnamese government

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:17.090
was committed to accepting
indefinite American aid

00:42:17.090 --> 00:42:19.490
on a large scale, including the import

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:22.350
of subsidized consumer
goods in order to generate

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:26.780
local piastre funds to
pay the army wage bill."

00:42:26.780 --> 00:42:30.320
Insofar as the Republican
army was the main beneficiary

00:42:30.320 --> 00:42:31.550
of the counterpart funds

00:42:31.550 --> 00:42:34.210
created by the sale of
consumer commodities,

00:42:34.210 --> 00:42:36.320
its existence was the main cause

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:37.893
of economic underdevelopment.

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:41.350
Against the urban elite and the Americans,

00:42:41.350 --> 00:42:44.030
the Ngos conceived of the
Strategic Hamlet Program,

00:42:44.030 --> 00:42:46.600
as a means of decentralizing the war

00:42:46.600 --> 00:42:48.500
by building up local militia

00:42:48.500 --> 00:42:50.440
while breaking the Republic's dependence

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:52.880
upon an economic assistance program

00:42:52.880 --> 00:42:55.900
that was perpetuating
the underdevelopment.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:58.303
As Nhu explained in an interview, quote,

00:42:59.557 --> 00:43:01.770
"My ambition is that the fortified hamlets

00:43:01.770 --> 00:43:05.180
may form a new approach better
than trying to get progress

00:43:05.180 --> 00:43:07.800
as a gift of the capitalist societies."

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:10.000
It is "a solution to the central problem

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:13.450
of underdevelopment in the 20th century."

00:43:13.450 --> 00:43:16.570
According to our report by a
Central Executive Committee

00:43:17.740 --> 00:43:20.870
of the People's Revolutionary Party,

00:43:20.870 --> 00:43:23.940
the solution would apply the same strategy

00:43:23.940 --> 00:43:26.480
as the one used by the insurgency,

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:28.380
initiating a social revolution

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:30.950
to establish a decentralized system

00:43:30.950 --> 00:43:35.627
of semi-autonomous rural
popular basis, quote,

00:43:35.627 --> 00:43:37.950
"by establishing strategic hamlets,

00:43:37.950 --> 00:43:39.910
the enemy would then have the conditions

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:42.540
for a mass manpower and material resources

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:45.880
to build additional forces,
increase military strength,

00:43:45.880 --> 00:43:48.530
employ the civil guard and militia forces

00:43:48.530 --> 00:43:50.087
to attack the revolution."

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:53.490
The solution to underdevelopment then

00:43:53.490 --> 00:43:56.040
was to carry out a far reaching program

00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:59.880
of social, political, and
military decentralization

00:43:59.880 --> 00:44:03.180
in order to both win the
war against the insurgency,

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:05.950
as well as to limit American interference

00:44:05.950 --> 00:44:08.220
using the leverage of economic aid.

00:44:08.220 --> 00:44:10.867
The Republic, as Nhu
explained to Maneli, quote,

00:44:10.867 --> 00:44:14.243
"is fighting against both the
guerrillas and imperialism.

00:44:15.100 --> 00:44:18.290
And to end on a speculative note,

00:44:18.290 --> 00:44:21.320
I'll suggest that it may have been

00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:22.940
because of the increasing success

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:24.833
of the Strategic Hamlet Campaign,

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:28.910
that was behind the Ngos' adamant refusal

00:44:28.910 --> 00:44:30.910
to liberalize their regime,

00:44:30.910 --> 00:44:33.350
even in the face of a
complete suspension of aid

00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:36.010
and a possible end to the US alliance.

00:44:36.010 --> 00:44:38.585
So according to a US
state department report,

00:44:38.585 --> 00:44:41.660
Nhu, quote, "will consider
present aid pressures

00:44:41.660 --> 00:44:43.890
as a welcome excuse to bring about changes

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:47.040
in the rural scene and
perhaps in the whole economy."

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:48.850
these changes will be based on his desire

00:44:48.850 --> 00:44:51.360
to get the Americans
out of the countryside,

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:54.650
in his belief that he has
a strong political base.

00:44:54.650 --> 00:44:57.760
Now, the fact that these
changes were supposed to create

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:01.030
a decentralized form of
socialism in the South,

00:45:01.030 --> 00:45:03.830
suggests that the war
during this early phase

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:07.140
was not a struggle between
communism and democracy

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:09.380
or nationalism and Marxism,

00:45:09.380 --> 00:45:11.870
rather it could perhaps
be better described

00:45:11.870 --> 00:45:14.440
as a struggle between two competing forms

00:45:14.440 --> 00:45:16.550
of anticolonial communism.

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:17.383
Thanks.

00:45:22.870 --> 00:45:23.790
- Thank you, Duy.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:27.860
That was a fantastic talk,
a lot to discuss here.

00:45:27.860 --> 00:45:31.900
So I'm going to open it up
to question and answer now.

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:36.000
If you can use the raise
hand function on Zoom,

00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:36.860
please use that.

00:45:36.860 --> 00:45:40.770
If you have trouble using
that, turn on your camera

00:45:40.770 --> 00:45:41.780
and start waving your hand,

00:45:41.780 --> 00:45:43.530
and I'll try to put you in a queue.

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:51.880
Well, people are thinking of questions.

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:53.230
I actually have a question.

00:45:54.258 --> 00:45:56.258
I have several, but I'll start with one.

00:45:57.891 --> 00:46:01.143
I'm curious, based on this talk,

00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:08.000
where the Ngos' concept
of the village comes from?

00:46:11.444 --> 00:46:13.890
You know, I'm an anthropologist,
and so I'm very interested

00:46:13.890 --> 00:46:17.550
in social organization and
those kinds of things, right?

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:19.670
And it seems to me in many ways

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:23.530
that there's a kind of
imagined understanding

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:26.463
of what the village
social organization is.

00:46:27.450 --> 00:46:31.230
And then when you showed that
image of the strategic hamlet,

00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:34.810
it reminded me of things
I had noticed in the past,

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:38.480
the circular organization of
the strategic hamlet itself

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:44.006
is very similar to a kind
of red river village form,

00:46:44.006 --> 00:46:46.710
going back in like careful studies

00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:48.860
of Vietnamese village organization,

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:52.880
there's a wide proliferation
of village organization,

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:56.800
and Southern Vietnamese
villages tend to be linear,

00:46:56.800 --> 00:46:58.190
whereas the ones in the North

00:46:58.190 --> 00:47:00.160
tend to be these kind of circular

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:03.130
behind the bamboo hedge, so to speak.

00:47:03.130 --> 00:47:08.130
And a lot of these ideas
about the emperor's law

00:47:08.620 --> 00:47:10.410
seeds to village custom,

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:12.330
these are all like sort of folk sayings

00:47:12.330 --> 00:47:13.490
that are quite popular,

00:47:13.490 --> 00:47:16.100
but they become political theory in a way.

00:47:16.100 --> 00:47:19.010
And so I'm just curious if you got a sense

00:47:19.010 --> 00:47:21.750
of where their concept of
the village comes from.

00:47:21.750 --> 00:47:25.510
And then a secondary question to that is,

00:47:25.510 --> 00:47:27.230
they're working in Saigon,

00:47:27.230 --> 00:47:30.410
developing these ideas at this time

00:47:30.410 --> 00:47:32.880
about a kind of local level democracy,

00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:35.680
which is very convincing the
way you put it in some sense,

00:47:35.680 --> 00:47:36.700
but did they have a theory

00:47:36.700 --> 00:47:38.963
of how that would play out in urban areas?

00:47:40.950 --> 00:47:42.860
- So the first question,

00:47:42.860 --> 00:47:46.620
I think the Ngos subscribed to parts

00:47:46.620 --> 00:47:48.770
of this kind of nationalist mythology

00:47:48.770 --> 00:47:52.210
about 2,000 year history
of the Vietnamese people

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:55.100
that was kind of invented
during the colonial period.

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:57.840
And so just as the communist in the North

00:47:57.840 --> 00:48:01.750
were trying to cast their
struggle against the Americans

00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:06.750
as the last kind of act
in this 2,000-year history

00:48:08.200 --> 00:48:11.060
of resistance against
all foreign aggression,

00:48:11.060 --> 00:48:15.780
the Ngos were trying to cast
the Strategic Hamlet Campaign

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:19.410
as the next stage in
this process of Nam Tien,

00:48:19.410 --> 00:48:24.400
or the southward advance,
which was this important part

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:25.540
of the national mythology

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:28.130
that was created during
the colonial period.

00:48:28.130 --> 00:48:30.420
And their idea was that
the strategic hamlets

00:48:30.420 --> 00:48:32.400
would not only be kind of,

00:48:32.400 --> 00:48:34.340
not just kind of a geographical expansion

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:38.580
of the form of the village as it was in...

00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:42.430
But it would also be kind of this advance

00:48:42.430 --> 00:48:45.955
into this non-Western
form of modernity, too.

00:48:45.955 --> 00:48:50.955
So that's would be my answer
to the first question.

00:48:51.280 --> 00:48:53.150
And then the second question.

00:48:53.150 --> 00:48:55.980
Yeah, the geography, I
think, in south Vietnam

00:48:56.930 --> 00:48:59.917
created a lot of problems for
the Strategic Hamlet Program,

00:48:59.917 --> 00:49:02.410
and it was because a lot of these villages

00:49:02.410 --> 00:49:05.300
were built along these canals

00:49:05.300 --> 00:49:09.830
that the program ended up
needing to kind of forcefully

00:49:10.900 --> 00:49:13.897
relocate peasants much more
than the Ngos had intended

00:49:13.897 --> 00:49:15.510
the program to do.

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:17.240
Different from the Agroville Program,

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:20.050
because they wouldn't have to
move so many people around.

00:49:20.050 --> 00:49:21.760
But I think because of the geography,

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:25.380
they ended up having to move
more than they anticipated.

00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:28.950
And then the last question
you had was about...

00:49:28.950 --> 00:49:29.940
Sorry, I'm.

00:49:32.611 --> 00:49:35.210
- What I was asking was about the urban,

00:49:35.210 --> 00:49:39.230
because this is kinda based on
a kinda an idealized version

00:49:39.230 --> 00:49:44.230
of some kind of village
democracy or something like that.

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:47.460
How did they expect that might
play out in an urban setting?

00:49:47.460 --> 00:49:50.266
Or maybe they hadn't thought it through?

00:49:50.266 --> 00:49:54.650
- I'm not sure, but they imagined
that the social revolution

00:49:54.650 --> 00:49:57.390
that they were initiating
in the countryside

00:49:57.390 --> 00:49:59.190
would come back and somehow revise

00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:00.740
the institutions in the cities.

00:50:02.040 --> 00:50:03.943
But I don't have much more than that.

00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:10.340
- We have a question from Charles

00:50:10.340 --> 00:50:12.190
and then after that Christoph Giebel.

00:50:14.340 --> 00:50:16.430
- Hi, Duy. Hi, everybody.

00:50:16.430 --> 00:50:18.163
Duy, thanks for a terrific talk.

00:50:19.770 --> 00:50:23.980
So I guess my question has
to do about the utility

00:50:23.980 --> 00:50:27.150
of ideological interpretations

00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:28.650
of the South Vietnamese state.

00:50:29.600 --> 00:50:33.838
I'm asking the question because
the south Vietnamese state,

00:50:33.838 --> 00:50:36.490
Goscha's used the term "archipelago state"

00:50:36.490 --> 00:50:37.830
to describe the DRV state

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:40.420
in early parts of the war against France.

00:50:40.420 --> 00:50:42.610
And I think the term is
useful in understanding

00:50:42.610 --> 00:50:44.723
the South Vietnamese state as well.

00:50:46.520 --> 00:50:47.630
I don't wanna call it weak,

00:50:47.630 --> 00:50:50.720
but institutionally highly uneven.

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:55.720
And I think more importantly
characterized always by a range

00:50:57.160 --> 00:51:01.400
of very diverse political
constituencies who

00:51:01.400 --> 00:51:03.880
might not have been sitting
in the presidential palace,

00:51:03.880 --> 00:51:07.683
but who exerted real political power.

00:51:09.600 --> 00:51:12.670
So, you know, given that,
I mean, and, you know,

00:51:12.670 --> 00:51:14.810
if you accept that characterization

00:51:14.810 --> 00:51:17.100
of the south Vietnamese state

00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:18.763
or of south Vietnamese politics,

00:51:20.580 --> 00:51:23.520
I just wonder, again, what the utility is

00:51:23.520 --> 00:51:27.093
of an ideological
interpretation of that regime.

00:51:28.950 --> 00:51:30.220
I mean, I guess this stems

00:51:30.220 --> 00:51:32.150
from me being a little bit uncomfortable

00:51:32.150 --> 00:51:35.973
with characterizing South Vietnam,

00:51:37.120 --> 00:51:42.120
as under the sort of umbrella
of the ideological vision

00:51:44.910 --> 00:51:46.320
that you just described, right?

00:51:46.320 --> 00:51:49.250
The fact that there were
constituencies in South Vietnam

00:51:49.250 --> 00:51:52.010
that were advocates of liberal democracy

00:51:52.010 --> 00:51:53.840
who didn't hold state power.

00:51:53.840 --> 00:51:56.270
Does that mean that we
should not include them

00:51:56.270 --> 00:51:58.017
in a definition of what south Vietnam was,

00:51:58.017 --> 00:52:01.070
and therefore by extension a
description of what the war was

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:04.420
ideologically between,
as you described it,

00:52:04.420 --> 00:52:09.420
two different competing
forms of communism, right?

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:12.210
And I guess my last point is that,

00:52:12.210 --> 00:52:13.930
I ask this question because I fear

00:52:13.930 --> 00:52:17.780
that ideological approaches
to understanding South Vietnam

00:52:17.780 --> 00:52:20.540
have, at least historically,
inevitably been

00:52:20.540 --> 00:52:23.720
kind of pulled seamlessly into

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:27.173
these kind of orthodox
revisionist debates,

00:52:28.010 --> 00:52:30.430
and I think have kind of
precluded in some ways

00:52:31.520 --> 00:52:34.300
deeper understanding of
South Vietnamese politics.

00:52:34.300 --> 00:52:37.253
So anyway, I'll kind of stop
there and see what you think.

00:52:39.470 --> 00:52:43.030
- Well, I think that
this ideological approach

00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:45.200
at least kind of gets us around

00:52:45.200 --> 00:52:48.010
this pervasive characterization
of the first Republic

00:52:48.010 --> 00:52:49.700
as pretending to be something

00:52:49.700 --> 00:52:51.610
that I don't think it
was pretending to be.

00:52:51.610 --> 00:52:53.050
But like you were saying,

00:52:53.050 --> 00:52:55.080
it did have to make so many compromises,

00:52:55.080 --> 00:52:56.400
both with the Americans

00:52:56.400 --> 00:52:59.270
and with all of these
other groups in the South

00:52:59.270 --> 00:53:02.040
taking on the trappings
of democratic government,

00:53:02.040 --> 00:53:04.630
in spite of the fact that
the Ngos were one group

00:53:04.630 --> 00:53:07.393
that repudiated that form of government.

00:53:08.240 --> 00:53:11.340
So, yeah.

00:53:11.340 --> 00:53:15.120
So I suppose, insofar as the
ideological interpretation

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:17.020
kind of covers over the
fact that those compromises

00:53:17.020 --> 00:53:19.220
had to be made with all
those groups though,

00:53:19.220 --> 00:53:21.260
then it's not helpful.

00:53:21.260 --> 00:53:22.283
I agree with that.

00:53:24.460 --> 00:53:26.550
But yeah, at the same time,

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:31.340
and also I think it's a significant

00:53:34.560 --> 00:53:38.440
reframing of the war because it's been

00:53:38.440 --> 00:53:39.610
this consensus largely

00:53:39.610 --> 00:53:40.810
that there are conservative government

00:53:40.810 --> 00:53:44.170
on both the orthodox and
the revisionist side.

00:53:44.170 --> 00:53:46.433
And, but yeah.

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:48.423
- Thanks.
- Sure.

00:53:50.800 --> 00:53:51.913
- Christoph, go ahead.

00:53:55.810 --> 00:53:56.730
- Hey, Duy.

00:53:58.750 --> 00:54:01.040
I think your book is probably

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:04.394
one of the most exciting interventions

00:54:04.394 --> 00:54:09.394
in modern Vietnamese historical
studies in a long time.

00:54:10.150 --> 00:54:10.983
- Oh, thanks.

00:54:10.983 --> 00:54:15.980
- So I'm very, very intrigued
by what you have produced

00:54:17.819 --> 00:54:20.233
and the arguments that you're making.

00:54:21.490 --> 00:54:23.780
And I'm speaking as somebody
who is not convinced

00:54:23.780 --> 00:54:27.880
by the dichotomy of
orthodoxy and revisionism

00:54:27.880 --> 00:54:30.090
when it comes to Vietnam studies.

00:54:30.090 --> 00:54:32.910
I think it applies to some
degree in Vietnam war studies,

00:54:32.910 --> 00:54:37.910
but I think it's misapplied
in Vietnam studies.

00:54:38.190 --> 00:54:43.190
I think there are other
orthodoxies, but not those.

00:54:44.470 --> 00:54:48.270
But in any case, kind of piggyback

00:54:48.270 --> 00:54:49.980
a little bit on Charles's...

00:54:51.430 --> 00:54:55.870
I think your argument is really
persuasive, it's intriguing

00:54:55.870 --> 00:54:59.163
when it comes to the thinking of the Ngos,

00:55:00.680 --> 00:55:04.790
but I'm wondering two things.

00:55:04.790 --> 00:55:07.520
One is how much can we
talk about South Vietnam

00:55:07.520 --> 00:55:11.690
as a unit, particularly after
the establishment of the NLF.

00:55:12.800 --> 00:55:17.800
And the preceding suppression
of older Viet Minh networks

00:55:20.460 --> 00:55:23.170
that must have riled up

00:55:23.170 --> 00:55:25.690
a lot of the kind of longstanding networks

00:55:25.690 --> 00:55:30.653
of revolutionary support
in the countryside.

00:55:34.750 --> 00:55:39.750
During the time that the
strategic cabinets are built up,

00:55:39.770 --> 00:55:43.820
but that is also the time
where the NLF is making

00:55:43.820 --> 00:55:46.180
amazing inroads in the countryside.

00:55:46.180 --> 00:55:50.720
By 1963, the NLF is projecting power

00:55:50.720 --> 00:55:53.730
into probably something like a third

00:55:53.730 --> 00:55:55.873
of the countryside south or the DMZ.

00:56:01.270 --> 00:56:04.330
I see your argument at
that ideological level

00:56:04.330 --> 00:56:08.530
and at the government level,

00:56:08.530 --> 00:56:12.110
but what other consequences on this,

00:56:12.110 --> 00:56:13.803
in the villages,

00:56:15.750 --> 00:56:17.810
where does it go wrong, so to say?

00:56:17.810 --> 00:56:22.810
I mean, I think this quote
from the central office

00:56:22.870 --> 00:56:25.330
of Southern Vietnam, I
think it's very intriguing,

00:56:25.330 --> 00:56:28.260
there there's clearly
nervousness about it,

00:56:28.260 --> 00:56:33.260
but still there is an amazing amount

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:38.680
of territory that changes hands
until 1963 and thereafter.

00:56:40.720 --> 00:56:45.210
And that would need to
be explained as well.

00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:46.400
Thank you.

00:56:46.400 --> 00:56:48.193
- Yeah. Thanks for that question.

00:56:49.970 --> 00:56:52.670
Yeah, I think your point seems to be

00:56:52.670 --> 00:56:56.610
a little bit like the point
that Charles Keith is making

00:56:56.610 --> 00:56:59.400
about taking this ideological view

00:56:59.400 --> 00:57:04.400
and how that covers over
a lot of important shifts.

00:57:04.660 --> 00:57:06.460
So if you read through the Ngos account

00:57:06.460 --> 00:57:09.130
and what's happening from
the beginning of the Republic

00:57:09.130 --> 00:57:10.000
until the very end though,

00:57:10.000 --> 00:57:11.570
is this sort of straight line story

00:57:11.570 --> 00:57:14.210
where they declare the
foundation of the Republic,

00:57:14.210 --> 00:57:17.090
it's a personalist democracy

00:57:17.090 --> 00:57:19.450
embraces this personalist
concept of democracy.

00:57:19.450 --> 00:57:22.510
And then they gradually sort
of realize their revolution

00:57:22.510 --> 00:57:23.930
when they figure out how to organize

00:57:23.930 --> 00:57:25.930
this Strategic Hamlet Campaign.

00:57:25.930 --> 00:57:27.280
And so it's kind of the straight line,

00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:29.473
but it sounds rather

00:57:34.300 --> 00:57:36.830
that they're not really
sure at the beginning

00:57:36.830 --> 00:57:41.153
how to carry out this
communitarian revolution

00:57:44.690 --> 00:57:46.030
that they have in mind.

00:57:46.030 --> 00:57:48.360
And they make a bunch of
compromises, like I was saying,

00:57:48.360 --> 00:57:50.893
to sort of their political
opponents in Vietnam.

00:57:53.540 --> 00:57:54.373
Sorry.

00:57:58.250 --> 00:58:00.750
Where, for example, they
don't carry out as radical

00:58:00.750 --> 00:58:02.410
the land reform as they
may have wanted though,

00:58:02.410 --> 00:58:06.220
because they felt at that point
they needed sort of members

00:58:06.220 --> 00:58:10.183
of this urban elite to
construct their government.

00:58:16.180 --> 00:58:17.930
So that later on, by 1962,

00:58:17.930 --> 00:58:19.720
they seem to kind of shift policies.

00:58:19.720 --> 00:58:22.190
And so you hear the minister
of the interior (indistinct)

00:58:22.190 --> 00:58:24.020
saying that we should have done

00:58:24.020 --> 00:58:25.160
what they did in the North though,

00:58:25.160 --> 00:58:27.550
and gotten rid of this class instead,

00:58:27.550 --> 00:58:29.761
because now it's creating
all of these problems.

00:58:29.761 --> 00:58:34.761
And so I agree that this
kind of ideological framing

00:58:35.770 --> 00:58:38.430
of the story that was excluding
all those important moments.

00:58:38.430 --> 00:58:40.460
But I do think that it's important to note

00:58:40.460 --> 00:58:42.810
that, I think, against the consensus story

00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:45.520
about why the Strategic
Hamlet Program fails.

00:58:45.520 --> 00:58:48.400
The causality that's usually given is that

00:58:48.400 --> 00:58:50.810
the program fails and that's
kind of the major catalyst

00:58:50.810 --> 00:58:52.730
for the collapse of the First Republic.

00:58:52.730 --> 00:58:55.240
But it seems from all
these different sources

00:58:55.240 --> 00:58:57.480
that the program doesn't
really sort of begin

00:58:57.480 --> 00:59:00.460
to completely stall until the coup occurs.

00:59:00.460 --> 00:59:03.230
And so I think the causality
there is the opposite.

00:59:03.230 --> 00:59:05.530
And it seems to me as if it
almost has to be this way,

00:59:05.530 --> 00:59:08.060
because the generals
essentially get into power,

00:59:08.060 --> 00:59:10.830
establish the revolutionary council,

00:59:10.830 --> 00:59:13.540
and they can't decide on
anything except for one item,

00:59:13.540 --> 00:59:15.800
and that is that they have to get rid

00:59:15.800 --> 00:59:17.290
of the Strategic Hamlet Campaign.

00:59:17.290 --> 00:59:20.660
So on November 2nd, they
go about dismantling

00:59:20.660 --> 00:59:23.140
some 800 of these hamlets though.

00:59:23.140 --> 00:59:25.310
And then the pace picks up after that.

00:59:25.310 --> 00:59:26.850
I think that what they're actually doing

00:59:26.850 --> 00:59:28.520
is essentially getting rid

00:59:28.520 --> 00:59:33.130
of what is this kind of front
line in this political war,

00:59:33.130 --> 00:59:34.430
that has no front lines, right?

00:59:34.430 --> 00:59:36.300
So these are all the local militia,

00:59:36.300 --> 00:59:37.850
the cadre at the village level

00:59:37.850 --> 00:59:41.700
that are trying to keep communist
cadre from infiltrating.

00:59:41.700 --> 00:59:43.490
And so yeah...

00:59:43.490 --> 00:59:48.490
And so, I guess, that's what
I have to say about that.

00:59:49.428 --> 00:59:52.340
- So can I follow up,
I mean, so why are they

00:59:52.340 --> 00:59:56.350
so laser focused on dismantling
the strategic hamlets

00:59:56.350 --> 00:59:58.700
November 2nd, 1963?

00:59:58.700 --> 00:59:59.893
That's amazing.

01:00:01.040 --> 01:00:03.537
I mean, is it that the school represents

01:00:03.537 --> 01:00:08.537
the very kind of urban minority class

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:13.260
that the goals are railing against

01:00:13.260 --> 01:00:15.735
are pushing themselves into power?

01:00:15.735 --> 01:00:19.370
I mean, why are they so focused

01:00:19.370 --> 01:00:21.573
on dismantling the strategic hamlets?

01:00:22.860 --> 01:00:25.350
- I think the development of
the Strategic Hamlet Program

01:00:25.350 --> 01:00:27.930
creates this sort of
antagonism between the Ngos

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:30.420
and the generals, because the
program is in part intended

01:00:30.420 --> 01:00:32.213
to limit the role of the military.

01:00:33.540 --> 01:00:34.600
And so...

01:00:40.610 --> 01:00:41.443
Yeah, I mean...

01:00:46.160 --> 01:00:49.897
And I think it partly has
to do too with the fact

01:00:51.560 --> 01:00:55.200
that the generals were
largely sort of excluded

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:57.260
from much of this program as well.

01:00:57.260 --> 01:00:59.170
But I guess, I think I'll
have to leave at that.

01:00:59.170 --> 01:01:00.950
Sorry, this is sort of material

01:01:00.950 --> 01:01:04.030
that's not fresh in my
mind right now, but...

01:01:07.650 --> 01:01:08.483
Thanks.

01:01:14.780 --> 01:01:17.970
Yeah, I'm sorry, like I'm in
the process of finishing a book

01:01:17.970 --> 01:01:19.390
on a totally different topic though.

01:01:19.390 --> 01:01:21.670
So part of this material sort
of not like in the forefront

01:01:21.670 --> 01:01:23.220
of my mind, unfortunately, but.

01:01:24.389 --> 01:01:27.210
- Duy, I wanted to ask another question

01:01:27.210 --> 01:01:31.893
circling back to sort of the
philosophical side of things.

01:01:32.940 --> 01:01:37.940
You had talked about Mounier's
philosophy of personalism

01:01:39.510 --> 01:01:44.510
and in almost every statement
about Mounier's philosophy,

01:01:44.910 --> 01:01:47.570
they always call him the Christian Marxist

01:01:47.570 --> 01:01:49.163
or the Catholic Marxist.

01:01:50.580 --> 01:01:53.920
And I wanted to take this
opportunity to learn from you

01:01:53.920 --> 01:01:57.460
about what exactly, when they say that

01:01:57.460 --> 01:01:59.090
on the book covers and those kind,

01:01:59.090 --> 01:02:01.650
what precisely about
it is considered to be

01:02:01.650 --> 01:02:05.710
the Catholic side of this
story, or the Christian Marxism,

01:02:05.710 --> 01:02:09.870
as opposed to a kind of
Marxist humanism, per se?

01:02:09.870 --> 01:02:10.703
- Right.

01:02:12.040 --> 01:02:15.450
Well, Mounier, I think,
believed that capitalism

01:02:15.450 --> 01:02:19.620
was this unchristian form of modernity,

01:02:19.620 --> 01:02:23.330
that somehow God had been supplemented

01:02:23.330 --> 01:02:27.570
by this kind of pagan
worship of these objects,

01:02:27.570 --> 01:02:30.363
that now had power over persons.

01:02:32.652 --> 01:02:34.500
But the funny thing about his personalism

01:02:34.500 --> 01:02:39.500
is I feel that it
reconciles in a lot of ways

01:02:41.170 --> 01:02:43.320
his Catholicism with his humanism.

01:02:43.320 --> 01:02:45.220
So the concept of the person

01:02:45.220 --> 01:02:50.220
occupies kind of the same
philosophical function as God.

01:02:52.590 --> 01:02:55.040
And so the argument that he makes,

01:02:55.040 --> 01:02:56.590
I think is something that like,

01:03:00.040 --> 01:03:04.180
God intended to put sort of
humans at the center of things

01:03:04.180 --> 01:03:06.593
because they're made in the image of God.

01:03:07.630 --> 01:03:09.600
So he didn't think that his humanism

01:03:09.600 --> 01:03:12.320
was in any way sort of in contradiction

01:03:12.320 --> 01:03:14.523
with his Christianity.

01:03:15.890 --> 01:03:18.490
The thing that I'm wondering about

01:03:18.490 --> 01:03:20.830
is whether this was the
case with Ngo Dinh Nhu,

01:03:20.830 --> 01:03:23.230
because in a lot of the
statements that he makes

01:03:25.401 --> 01:03:29.370
he seems to be kind of rejecting
Mounier's Christianity,

01:03:29.370 --> 01:03:33.723
while adopting this radical
part of his politics.

01:03:38.650 --> 01:03:42.140
- Just building on that part,
you know, the standard refrain

01:03:42.140 --> 01:03:43.450
in a lot of Vietnamese studies

01:03:43.450 --> 01:03:47.520
is to talk about the importance for Ngos

01:03:47.520 --> 01:03:50.210
of their Catholith or their Christianity.

01:03:50.210 --> 01:03:53.720
Are you arguing in that sense
that that's the one thing

01:03:53.720 --> 01:03:55.760
they're not interested in with Mounier?

01:03:55.760 --> 01:03:57.680
I think a lot of people
would make the assumption

01:03:57.680 --> 01:04:01.430
that it's because he's a
Catholic Christian philosopher

01:04:01.430 --> 01:04:05.330
of Marxism that he's
actually somewhat acceptable,

01:04:05.330 --> 01:04:07.560
but is that a totally wrongheaded reading

01:04:07.560 --> 01:04:11.197
of their attraction to
Mounier's personalism?

01:04:13.330 --> 01:04:15.550
- I think he was different
for each of the Ngos,

01:04:15.550 --> 01:04:18.000
but for Nhu clearly the attraction

01:04:18.000 --> 01:04:20.233
was less the Christian
side of it, I think though,

01:04:20.233 --> 01:04:21.803
than the political side.

01:04:23.240 --> 01:04:26.030
And so that's why he makes
these statements to the effect

01:04:26.030 --> 01:04:28.373
that there's no relationship
between my personalism

01:04:28.373 --> 01:04:31.100
and the personalism taught
by Catholic institutions

01:04:31.100 --> 01:04:34.370
in France and in Vietnam.

01:04:34.370 --> 01:04:37.340
But I'm not sure if it
was the same for Diem

01:04:37.340 --> 01:04:38.690
and for the other brothers.

01:04:40.394 --> 01:04:42.450
But I do think that the personalism

01:04:42.450 --> 01:04:44.730
that they were trying to
put into practice was...

01:04:44.730 --> 01:04:47.030
So one thing that I don't
focus very much in the book

01:04:47.030 --> 01:04:49.190
is this whole kind of body of literature,

01:04:49.190 --> 01:04:50.570
these pamphlets that were put out though

01:04:50.570 --> 01:04:52.770
for the purpose of
popularizing personalism.

01:04:53.936 --> 01:04:54.803
And I didn't like these very much,

01:04:54.803 --> 01:04:57.420
because they kind of remind me of books

01:04:57.420 --> 01:04:59.610
you can still find today
on Ho Chi Minh thought,

01:04:59.610 --> 01:05:00.930
where they make these claims

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:04.010
that personalism combines
all of the best elements

01:05:04.010 --> 01:05:06.680
of religions in Vietnam and in the West,

01:05:06.680 --> 01:05:08.870
so the best of Christianity
and the best of Confucianism

01:05:08.870 --> 01:05:09.840
and so forth,

01:05:09.840 --> 01:05:12.340
and what they don't give you
is sort of this more rigorous

01:05:12.340 --> 01:05:14.740
sort of philosophical arguments,
this political philosophy,

01:05:14.740 --> 01:05:18.303
that I think the Ngos were
trying to put into practice.

01:05:19.190 --> 01:05:21.510
But yeah, there is that whole
kind of religious dimension

01:05:21.510 --> 01:05:23.500
that a lot of scholars
that work on personalism

01:05:23.500 --> 01:05:26.037
have paid a lot more
attention than I have.

01:05:31.148 --> 01:05:36.148
- We have a question from (indistinct).

01:05:36.719 --> 01:05:39.730
- Thank you for this (indistinct).

01:05:39.730 --> 01:05:44.730
I have questions: in some of your slide,

01:05:45.330 --> 01:05:49.070
you talk about Ngo Dinh Nhu,

01:05:49.070 --> 01:05:53.730
he said about the underdevelopment
of Vietnam at that time.

01:05:53.730 --> 01:05:57.720
And to change it, they need
to change the structure

01:05:57.720 --> 01:05:58.843
is very Marxist,

01:06:00.060 --> 01:06:02.101
but during (indistinct),

01:06:02.101 --> 01:06:04.890
I don't know if I miss it,

01:06:04.890 --> 01:06:09.136
but he said about like
changing the superstructure

01:06:09.136 --> 01:06:13.950
and underdevelopment
is related to a lot...

01:06:13.950 --> 01:06:15.440
And they talk about capitalism.

01:06:15.440 --> 01:06:20.440
And so, the changes that
they want to learn new

01:06:22.250 --> 01:06:26.523
and the conflict in their political powers

01:06:27.760 --> 01:06:30.820
is about communism and democracy.

01:06:30.820 --> 01:06:33.040
And they didn't say anything

01:06:33.040 --> 01:06:36.793
about economic strategy or something.

01:06:38.611 --> 01:06:42.346
So maybe you didn't say
here, but in your research,

01:06:42.346 --> 01:06:46.787
in your book, in another
part of your book,

01:06:46.787 --> 01:06:50.480
did you mention anything
about the superstructure

01:06:50.480 --> 01:06:53.390
like about economic
development at that time,

01:06:53.390 --> 01:06:56.561
because they are under colony...

01:06:56.561 --> 01:06:57.911
I'm not sure it's a colony.

01:06:59.630 --> 01:07:01.600
They don't do anything for economic,

01:07:01.600 --> 01:07:05.070
they live on the edge of the US.

01:07:05.070 --> 01:07:10.070
So that's why they own about politics,

01:07:10.150 --> 01:07:15.150
nothing about economics,
and the (indistinct) people,

01:07:15.630 --> 01:07:19.310
because of that, they
just focus on politics

01:07:19.310 --> 01:07:22.930
and the politics is
not just in the urbans,

01:07:22.930 --> 01:07:25.180
in the center, in Saigon,

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:30.180
so that's why the rural was
match in the discussion,

01:07:31.260 --> 01:07:33.923
political discussion about the strategy.

01:07:33.923 --> 01:07:37.090
I'm going to learn from you. I don't know.

01:07:37.090 --> 01:07:40.670
I'm not sure I missed it because
it's a lot of philosophies.

01:07:40.670 --> 01:07:41.503
- Yeah.

01:07:42.940 --> 01:07:45.390
See, well, the Strategic Hamlet Program

01:07:45.390 --> 01:07:47.050
was really multifaceted program,

01:07:47.050 --> 01:07:50.780
but a big, big part of that was
not political, but economic.

01:07:50.780 --> 01:07:53.873
It was a rural economic
development program.

01:07:53.873 --> 01:07:56.050
And I think actually the Ngos

01:07:56.050 --> 01:07:57.710
actually thought a lot about economics.

01:07:57.710 --> 01:07:59.810
And I deal with this in the book,

01:07:59.810 --> 01:08:03.190
but there's also a lot of
recent research on the Ngos

01:08:03.190 --> 01:08:04.566
and how they tried to
deal with the problems

01:08:04.566 --> 01:08:08.780
of the commodity import program,
the American aid program.

01:08:08.780 --> 01:08:13.003
And so I think for the
Ngos, they recognized,

01:08:14.680 --> 01:08:16.980
maybe too late, right, That
the program was creating

01:08:16.980 --> 01:08:18.850
this kind of (indistinct)
class in the South,

01:08:18.850 --> 01:08:23.010
this oligarchy of wealthy
importers of US commodities,

01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:25.170
and that these commodities
were coming into the country

01:08:25.170 --> 01:08:28.560
and competing with local goods
and undermining industry.

01:08:28.560 --> 01:08:30.060
And then those complained about this

01:08:30.060 --> 01:08:31.330
to the Americans though.

01:08:31.330 --> 01:08:32.830
So some of the Vietnamese research on this

01:08:32.830 --> 01:08:34.930
is really interesting because they compare

01:08:35.920 --> 01:08:38.130
the effect of the aid program in Vietnam

01:08:38.130 --> 01:08:41.620
with a similar aid program in Korea.

01:08:41.620 --> 01:08:44.290
And the Koreans were dealing
with the same problem as well,

01:08:44.290 --> 01:08:46.993
though, where all of these
imported goods though

01:08:46.993 --> 01:08:50.190
was making it difficult to industrialize.

01:08:50.190 --> 01:08:52.840
And the way that the Koreans managed

01:08:52.840 --> 01:08:55.230
to get out of this
situation was by offering

01:08:55.230 --> 01:08:57.600
to sort of support the
US mission in Vietnam.

01:08:57.600 --> 01:09:01.050
And that's when they got
to renegotiate the program,

01:09:01.050 --> 01:09:04.430
so that more capital investment
was coming into the country.

01:09:04.430 --> 01:09:06.180
And at that point, though,

01:09:06.180 --> 01:09:09.360
as the war in Vietnam was beginning,

01:09:09.360 --> 01:09:10.950
they began to more rapidly develop,

01:09:10.950 --> 01:09:12.780
but they were having
similar problems in Vietnam.

01:09:12.780 --> 01:09:14.330
And so I think that was part of the reason

01:09:14.330 --> 01:09:17.690
why the threat of a
complete suspension of aid

01:09:17.690 --> 01:09:19.940
didn't sort of influence the Ngos

01:09:19.940 --> 01:09:22.456
in terms of liberalizing
their regime, though,

01:09:22.456 --> 01:09:25.720
as much as the Americans thought it would.

01:09:25.720 --> 01:09:27.810
But I think they thought a lot
about economic development.

01:09:27.810 --> 01:09:30.750
I think they thought sort of
development was actually key

01:09:31.700 --> 01:09:34.370
to winning the war.

01:09:34.370 --> 01:09:37.150
And that sort of the American approach

01:09:37.150 --> 01:09:39.830
of kind of like providing
all of these goods

01:09:39.830 --> 01:09:44.830
to the cities that weren't
making it out to the countryside

01:09:45.300 --> 01:09:49.450
and building up this huge army
and liberalizing the regime,

01:09:49.450 --> 01:09:54.290
would simply continue to
lead to underdevelopment

01:09:54.290 --> 01:09:55.423
in South Vietnam.

01:09:56.307 --> 01:09:57.140
But yeah.

01:09:59.340 --> 01:10:00.173
- Great.

01:10:00.173 --> 01:10:02.020
We have a question from Jerry Silverman.

01:10:02.020 --> 01:10:04.040
After that question, I'm gonna stop,

01:10:04.040 --> 01:10:06.370
after you answer that question,
I'll stop the recording,

01:10:06.370 --> 01:10:09.600
but I will keep the Zoom open
for a more informal chat.

01:10:09.600 --> 01:10:10.880
So Christoph, if that's okay,

01:10:10.880 --> 01:10:14.160
we'll have you ask your question
during the informal chat.

01:10:14.160 --> 01:10:15.003
Go ahead, Jerry.

01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:20.450
We'll flip that.

01:10:20.450 --> 01:10:23.280
And Christoph, if you can
ask your question and then-

01:10:23.280 --> 01:10:24.230
- [Jerry] Not mute.

01:10:27.300 --> 01:10:28.830
- Go ahead, Jerry. We can hear you.

01:10:28.830 --> 01:10:30.110
- [Jerry] Okay. I'm sorry.

01:10:30.110 --> 01:10:30.943
- That's okay.

01:10:30.943 --> 01:10:32.983
- [Jerry] I'm getting
used to the technology.

01:10:34.740 --> 01:10:37.450
As a political scientist,
I'm also interested

01:10:37.450 --> 01:10:42.450
in the nature of the
structure for governance

01:10:44.570 --> 01:10:45.863
at the village level,

01:10:47.030 --> 01:10:49.850
and whether or not you're talking about

01:10:49.850 --> 01:10:51.580
closed villages in the North,

01:10:51.580 --> 01:10:54.870
which I also agree is a
very different situation

01:10:54.870 --> 01:10:58.223
than in the Southern Delta,
for example, on rivers.

01:11:00.350 --> 01:11:02.640
At some level, you have a community,

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:07.633
even if it's in a linear sense,

01:11:08.980 --> 01:11:11.300
it may instead of a village or a hamlet,

01:11:11.300 --> 01:11:13.170
it might be a cluster of families,

01:11:13.170 --> 01:11:17.030
but somewhere there's
an authority structure

01:11:17.030 --> 01:11:21.363
that, quote, "governs" the entity.

01:11:22.770 --> 01:11:26.560
And I've been playing with the notion

01:11:26.560 --> 01:11:31.560
that the 1929 global depression

01:11:33.020 --> 01:11:36.340
and an impact on that social structure

01:11:37.780 --> 01:11:42.780
and that traditional elite
leadership at the village level

01:11:44.600 --> 01:11:49.600
was alienated from the village,
prior to the revolution,

01:11:50.300 --> 01:11:55.293
they were integrated into
the called capitalist system,

01:11:56.490 --> 01:11:59.030
sending their children to
school in the district,

01:11:59.030 --> 01:12:00.113
things like that.

01:12:01.390 --> 01:12:03.463
And that as that happens,

01:12:04.450 --> 01:12:08.793
non-formal leadership replaces that.

01:12:10.954 --> 01:12:15.497
And that's a major social
revolution in and of itself.

01:12:17.080 --> 01:12:21.460
And that accepting everything
that you're arguing

01:12:21.460 --> 01:12:26.460
with respect to the Ngos
political philosophy and intent,

01:12:29.190 --> 01:12:32.450
they were just way too late

01:12:33.750 --> 01:12:36.770
because the situation
coming out of the '30s

01:12:37.880 --> 01:12:42.880
had already created a, quote,
"modernized version" of life,

01:12:45.230 --> 01:12:48.803
of political authority
structures at the village.

01:12:50.400 --> 01:12:55.400
And therefore the war was
truly about governance

01:12:57.340 --> 01:12:58.583
at the village level.

01:12:59.700 --> 01:13:02.740
And that's where the competition was.

01:13:02.740 --> 01:13:06.800
But that the NLF based
on the earlier Viet Mihn

01:13:06.800 --> 01:13:11.800
had already started, you know,

01:13:12.550 --> 01:13:17.080
they recognized that new
emerging village elite

01:13:18.760 --> 01:13:21.180
as being the authority,

01:13:21.180 --> 01:13:24.890
and the French and then the First Republic

01:13:24.890 --> 01:13:27.073
did not recognize that authority.

01:13:28.600 --> 01:13:30.650
Now that's speculative.

01:13:30.650 --> 01:13:33.783
And you'll forgive me for
taking you back to the '30s,

01:13:34.900 --> 01:13:37.820
but I wonder if you have
any reaction to that.

01:13:40.984 --> 01:13:43.843
- Yeah, who's did backtrack?

01:13:50.903 --> 01:13:52.236
Let me see that.

01:13:53.506 --> 01:13:57.100
So I think the program of democratization,

01:13:57.100 --> 01:13:59.120
the way it was supposed to work

01:13:59.120 --> 01:14:03.840
is that instead of giving the population

01:14:03.840 --> 01:14:05.340
the ability to vote

01:14:05.340 --> 01:14:09.926
for powerful interest in people in Saigon

01:14:09.926 --> 01:14:12.350
once every few years,

01:14:12.350 --> 01:14:16.000
the idea was basically
if you can give people

01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:19.270
some measure of democratic
control over things

01:14:19.270 --> 01:14:22.360
that mattered immediately to
them, including their security

01:14:22.360 --> 01:14:26.350
and which development project
they wanted to engage in,

01:14:26.350 --> 01:14:29.590
that you can motivate
them politically much more

01:14:29.590 --> 01:14:32.937
than if you simply extended
electoral democracy.

01:14:35.703 --> 01:14:37.930
And this is partly why the Ngos
were describing this program

01:14:40.770 --> 01:14:42.149
as a social revolution as well,

01:14:42.149 --> 01:14:44.023
because they wanted to get rid of,

01:14:45.190 --> 01:14:48.430
they thought that by implementing
these democratic elections

01:14:48.430 --> 01:14:51.490
and allowing the population to get rid of

01:14:51.490 --> 01:14:56.450
kind of existing groups with privileges,

01:14:56.450 --> 01:15:01.450
that they can motivate the
population to organize itself.

01:15:06.060 --> 01:15:07.810
I'm sorry, I don't know
enough about this moment

01:15:07.810 --> 01:15:11.927
in the '30s that you're
talking about, when sort of...

01:15:11.927 --> 01:15:14.310
Are you saying then that sort
of the colonial structures

01:15:14.310 --> 01:15:16.170
of administration had
broken down at that point

01:15:16.170 --> 01:15:21.170
and you already have kind of a
modernized village structures

01:15:22.030 --> 01:15:23.343
at that stage?

01:15:24.520 --> 01:15:27.500
- Well, if I could
respond just very quickly,

01:15:27.500 --> 01:15:29.223
no, I'm really talking about,

01:15:30.760 --> 01:15:33.123
well, what I call parallel governance,

01:15:34.040 --> 01:15:39.040
that I accept the notion that
the traditional Vietnamese

01:15:40.563 --> 01:15:45.563
was that villages, hamlets,
I would go so far as families

01:15:47.380 --> 01:15:48.803
could govern themselves,

01:15:51.090 --> 01:15:53.450
but that the structure of authority

01:15:53.450 --> 01:15:57.630
within that context had shifted.

01:15:57.630 --> 01:15:58.590
- Right.

01:15:58.590 --> 01:16:00.460
- [Jerry] From the traditional elite

01:16:01.610 --> 01:16:05.700
to the people who emerged to the degree

01:16:05.700 --> 01:16:10.700
that the traditional elite had
to some degree abandoned them

01:16:11.700 --> 01:16:16.420
because of the depression,
they had a choice.

01:16:16.420 --> 01:16:19.870
They could either choose
living in the district

01:16:21.270 --> 01:16:25.640
or they could choose continuing
their role in the village.

01:16:25.640 --> 01:16:27.960
And many of them chose the district

01:16:27.960 --> 01:16:29.410
because they had already bought

01:16:29.410 --> 01:16:32.943
into the French colonial economic system.

01:16:34.240 --> 01:16:35.933
The elites in the village.

01:16:37.110 --> 01:16:42.110
And without those elites,
new leadership had to emerge.

01:16:44.010 --> 01:16:48.850
And my speculation is that it
emerged within that village

01:16:48.850 --> 01:16:51.100
from among people who were still there

01:16:51.967 --> 01:16:56.397
and that the communists recognized that

01:16:57.770 --> 01:17:02.283
and co-opted that new
leadership and recognized it,

01:17:03.170 --> 01:17:07.697
and the French colonial
system then, the Diem,

01:17:07.697 --> 01:17:10.220
did not recognize it.

01:17:10.220 --> 01:17:14.340
So that even though there may
have been parallel attempts

01:17:14.340 --> 01:17:18.950
to democratize the system
down to the village level,

01:17:18.950 --> 01:17:21.163
at least in rhetoric,

01:17:22.580 --> 01:17:27.580
that the Ngos were just
20 years or so too late.

01:17:31.880 --> 01:17:34.470
- So it may well be
that they were too late

01:17:34.470 --> 01:17:37.200
in beginning of this program,
but I think they did recognize

01:17:37.200 --> 01:17:39.160
the problem because one
of the explicit aims

01:17:39.160 --> 01:17:41.730
of the Strategic Hamlet
Program was to kind of build up

01:17:41.730 --> 01:17:46.250
this local elite as a
kind of counterbalance

01:17:46.250 --> 01:17:50.650
to what the Ngos perceived this
sort of French trained elite

01:17:50.650 --> 01:17:55.650
in the cities that had
kind of been trained

01:17:56.950 --> 01:17:59.470
in this like centralized
form of government

01:17:59.470 --> 01:18:01.380
and weren't capable of acting politically

01:18:01.380 --> 01:18:02.910
and autonomously and so forth, though.

01:18:02.910 --> 01:18:04.750
So they were trying to sort of recreate

01:18:04.750 --> 01:18:05.600
what the communist were doing

01:18:05.600 --> 01:18:08.110
in terms of building up
this local elite as well,

01:18:08.110 --> 01:18:10.700
but it may have been the fact...

01:18:10.700 --> 01:18:12.510
But they were starting very late with it.

01:18:12.510 --> 01:18:17.510
And I'm sure that was a
difficult problem to overcome.

01:18:20.150 --> 01:18:21.633
- [Jerry] Thank you very much.

01:18:22.500 --> 01:18:25.740
- So that's the last question
of our formal discussion.

01:18:25.740 --> 01:18:28.530
So before we switch to the
informal session, everyone,

01:18:28.530 --> 01:18:31.620
please join me in thanking
Duy for a fantastic talk.

01:18:31.620 --> 01:18:33.057
- Yeah. Thanks for showing up, everybody.

01:18:33.057 --> 01:18:34.007
- (applauding) Q&amp;A.

01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:36.870
I will stop the recording.

